{{define "privacy.gohtml"}}<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Privacy · WISEN</title>
<meta name="description" content="What the WISEN school platform collects, why, who can see it, how long it is kept, and how to ask for it to be corrected or erased.">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/app.css?v={{.AssetVersion}}">
{{template "themeboot"}}
<style>
  /* The auth card is sized for a form. A notice is prose, so it gets a
     reading width and the card's own edge, nothing else. */
  .auth main.card.notice { max-width: 46rem; text-align: left; }
  .notice h1 { margin-bottom: .25rem; }
  .notice h2 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 1.6rem 0 .4rem; }
  .notice p, .notice li { line-height: 1.55; }
  .notice ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .notice .meta { opacity: .7; font-size: .9rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .notice table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .95rem; margin: .5rem 0 1rem; }
  .notice th, .notice td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: .45rem .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(128,128,128,.25); }
</style>
</head>
<body class="auth">
  <div class="auth-top">{{template "themebtn"}}</div>
  <main class="card notice">
    <h1>Privacy notice</h1>
    <p class="meta">Last updated {{.Updated}} · Written to the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023</p>

    <p>WISEN is the software a school uses to run its office: admissions, fees,
    attendance, exams, transport and messages to families. This notice says what
    the platform holds about you or your child, why, who can see it, how long it
    stays, and what you can ask for.</p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p><strong>Your school</strong> decides what is collected and why. In the words of
    the Act it is the <em>data fiduciary</em>. Questions about your own or your
    child's records go to the school office first.</p>
    <p><strong>WISEN</strong>, the company that makes and hosts the platform, processes
    that data on the school's instructions and does not use it for anything
    else. It is the <em>data processor</em>.
    {{if .SupportEmail}}You can reach it at <a href="mailto:{{.SupportEmail}}">{{.SupportEmail}}</a>.{{end}}</p>

    <h2>What is collected, and why</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>About</th><th>What</th><th>Why</th></tr>
      <tr><td>A student</td><td>Name, date of birth, class, photo, admission number, attendance, marks, health notes the family gives, bus stop</td><td>To run the school year: registers, report cards, safety on the bus</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A parent or guardian</td><td>Name, relation, phone, email, occupation and annual income where declared for a concession</td><td>To reach you, to sign you in, and to work out fee concessions and RTE eligibility</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fees</td><td>Invoices, receipts, the last four digits of a payment reference. Never a card number or a UPI PIN — payment happens in your bank's app</td><td>Billing and receipts the school must keep for its accounts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Staff</td><td>Employment, attendance, pay, statutory identifiers the law requires (PAN, UAN)</td><td>Payroll and statutory filings</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Identity documents</td><td>Aadhaar is stored only as its last four digits, and only when a scheme the school takes part in requires it</td><td>Government schemes; never for sign-in, never in the audit log</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Use of the app</td><td>Sign-ins (time, device, approximate location from the network), pages opened, what was changed and by whom</td><td>Security, and an audit trail a school can show an inspector</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h2>Who can see it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>School staff</strong>, each only what their role needs. A class teacher sees their class; the accounts desk sees fees, not marks.</li>
      <li><strong>You</strong>, for your own child. A school may limit alerts about marks and fees to the primary guardian on record, and may end a guardian's access when custody or enrolment changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Message providers</strong> (SMS, WhatsApp, email) receive the message and your number or address, to deliver it. Nothing else.</li>
      <li><strong>Payment providers</strong> receive the amount and a reference when you choose to pay online.</li>
      <li><strong>WISEN support staff</strong> can reach a school's records only during a support session the school can see in its own audit trail.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The platform does not sell data, does not show advertising, and does not
    use your child's data to train anything.</p>

    <h2>Where it is kept</h2>
    <p>On servers in India (Google Cloud, Mumbai region). Backups are encrypted.
    Files a school uploads (photos, certificates) are stored in the same region.</p>

    <h2>How long</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Academic and fee records: for as long as the school is required to keep them, which for most Indian boards and for tax is at least eight years after the year they concern.</li>
      <li>Messages and the audit trail: kept by the school; the school can ask for a retention period to be applied.</li>
      <li>Sign-in sessions: ended automatically after a period of inactivity; the device list is kept for ninety days.</li>
      <li>An enquiry that never became an admission: erased after the admission season unless you asked to be contacted again.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>Under the Act you may ask the school to show you what it holds about you or
    your child, to correct it, to erase what it no longer needs, and to name
    someone to act for you. You may withdraw consent for anything that was based
    on consent — for example, a photograph on the school wall — without it
    affecting your child's schooling. Ask the school office; the school will
    answer within thirty days. If you are not satisfied, you may complain to the
    Data Protection Board of India.</p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>A child's record is created and controlled by the school with the consent
    of a parent or guardian. Students who sign in themselves see only their own
    work. The platform does not track children's behaviour for advertising or
    profiling.</p>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>Every school's data is separated from every other's at the database level.
    Traffic is encrypted. Passwords are stored as salted, peppered hashes and are
    never shown or sent after the first issue. Staff sign-ins can require a
    second factor; a wrong password locks the account for five minutes after
    eight attempts.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>When this notice changes, the date at the top changes with it and the
    school is told. The version in force is always the one at this address.</p>

    <p class="fineprint"><a href="/login">Back to sign in</a></p>
  </main>
{{template "themejs"}}
</body>
</html>{{end}}
